Subject: Re: App integration velocity handshake — technical notes\n
Alex / team — quick technical notes ahead of tomorrow's sync. Pasted below is a compact integration checklist and a minimal sample showing retry/backoff and endpoint selection.

Integration checklist (top priorities):
1) API keys & token rotation: issue short-lived API tokens (TTL 6 hours) with optional refresh-token flow for long-lived services. Our recommend: rotate tokens every 6h for worker processes, 1h for browser sessions. We support JWTs via your KMS.
2) Network & TLS: enforce TLS1.2+, validate SNI. Allow egress to us-west-2.redwood-api.com and us-east-1.redwood-api.com (see attached diagram).
3) Rate limits & throttles: default per-account QPS=50 with burst window 200; we expose X-RateLimit-* headers to help your client adapt. For pilot, request temporary lift to QPS=200 with a 2x burst.
4) Migration: start with SDK in parallel mode — mirror requests to both endpoints (CardinalPay -> Redwood staging) and compare responses. After 72h of parity, switch prod DNS and monitor errors for 24h.

Minimal sample (Node.js like pseudocode — in SDK bundle as well):
const requestWithRetry = async (opts) => {
  const maxAttempts = 5;
  let attempt = 0;
  while (attempt < maxAttempts) {
    try {
      const res = await fetch(opts.url, { method: 'POST', headers: opts.headers, body: opts.body, timeout: 10000 });
      if (res.status >= 500) throw new Error('server');
      return await res.json();
    } catch (e) {
      attempt++;
      if (attempt >= maxAttempts) throw e;
      const backoff = Math.min(8000, 200 * Math.pow(2, attempt));
      await sleep(backoff + jitter());
    }
  }
};

Notes on webhooks: for sub-second synchronous webhooks, avoid buffering: respond with 202 immediately and do an async verification when possible. If you must synchronous-call Redwood, keep timeouts <=1s and mark the endpoint as high-priority to route to low-latency instances (we can enable a low-latency path for your account during pilot).

Observability & alerts: enable request tracing headers (X-Request-ID) so Redwood can correlate traces. We'll provide a dashboard in Console showing per-route latency percentiles and error rates.

